{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-30330",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/021a84ea0ad36014569028c0ffb39ab9b30f9225cf4bfcccba2d60050754a6f9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between Harry and the cat, while $y$ is the length of the cat's shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the velocity of the cat's shadow\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the velocity of the cat's shadow is *negative* because the cat is walking *toward* Harry, and therefore the distance between Harry and the cat *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{30} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =30y \\\\ 10x & =20y \\\\ x & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2(-11)=-22\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cat is $22\\,\\text{in/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
